Change 27 is still invented, and by now you know it well: the one-line fix to a confusing sentence customers saw after sending money abroad. In the [last essay](/blog/the-one-number-a-board-should-watch), it finally showed up where a board could see it, as a dot on a lead-time chart, forty-five days from decision to customer. Somebody at the board table asked the right question.

{% answerup /%}

"Resistance to change" is a satisfying answer, because it's about other people. It's also usually wrong. Follow change 27 through the chairs it passed on its way to customers, and you won't find anyone lazy or afraid of technology. You'll find four sensible people, each making the safest choice the rules allowed.

*This is the fourth essay in a series on digital transformation in banking. [The first](/blog/the-factory-is-the-easy-part) ended on people: fear of change is usually rational. This one is about the arithmetic behind it.*

## Four chairs, one change

{% fourchairs /%}

Look at what each of them is measured on, and every delay makes sense. None of it is a character flaw. W. Edwards Deming, who spent a career on this question, put a number on it.

{% statgrid value=94 label="belong to the system, which is management's responsibility" restLabel="come from special causes" title="Deming's estimate, per 100 troubles and chances to improve" source="W. Edwards Deming · Out of the Crisis, 1986" /%}

## The gatekeeper's arithmetic

Half a century ago, the management scholar Steven Kerr named the pattern in a paper whose title says it all.

{% hopereward /%}

Banks hope for speed, and reward caution. Add one more fact about people: we feel losses about twice as strongly as equal gains, according to a [2024 meta-analysis](https://doi.org/10.1257/jel.20221698) of more than six hundred estimates. Now put yourself in the chair of anyone who has to say yes to change 27, and run the numbers.

{% gatekeepermath /%}

Under the old rules, holding never loses, however safe the change. Fear of change isn't a mood. It's arithmetic. And a speed target on top of it only moves the changes nobody was worried about.

{% pullquote quote="You can't bribe your way past blame. You have to remove it." /%}

The rules that remove it are already in this series, and each one fixes a cell.

{% rulekit /%}

## What happens after something breaks

No rewrite of the rules survives the wrong answer to one question: what does the organisation do the first time a fast change goes wrong?

The sociologist Ron Westrum sorted organisations by how they handle information: *pathological*, oriented around power; *bureaucratic*, around rules; and *generative*, around performance. [DORA's research](https://dora.dev/capabilities/generative-organizational-culture/) finds that a generative culture predicts software delivery and organisational performance. Its [2019 report](https://dora.dev/research/2019/dora-report/) found the same for psychological safety, Amy Edmondson's term for a team's shared belief that it's safe to take interpersonal risks.

{% messengertest /%}

Culture isn't the values on the wall. It's what people learned the last time something broke. That's why I've come to think the most important meeting in a transformation isn't the steering committee. It's the first incident review after a fast change fails. Sidney Dekker calls the alternative to blame a just culture: accountability that asks what happened and what needs repairing, rather than who should be punished.

## Look closer

When the diagnosis is "culture", look one level down.

{% lookcloser /%}

The first essay put it plainly: you can't ask people to change their mindset while their processes still punish the new behaviour.

## Where to start

{% steps %}
1. **Follow one real change through the chairs.** Pick your own change 27 and sit with each person who touched it. Ask what they're measured on, not why they were slow.
2. **Find the asymmetry.** For each wait, ask what would have happened to that person if they'd moved faster and it had gone wrong, and what would have happened if it had gone right.
3. **Make failure small before asking for speed.** Small releases, automatic rollback, evidence by default. Fear shrinks with the worst case.
4. **Rewrite one rule a quarter.** Start with the rule that punishes the people closest to the customer.
5. **Make the next incident review the example.** People will judge the transformation by how it treats the first fast change that fails, far more than by any town hall.
{% /steps %}

## What I'd tell a board

{% boardcard title="Five things to know about resistance to change" %}
1. **Resistance is information.** It shows you where your rules make speed dangerous for the person who has to say yes.
2. **Look at the payoffs before the people.** If speed earns nothing and failure costs a career, slow is the rational choice.
3. **You can't bribe your way past blame.** A speed target on top of a blame culture only moves the changes nobody was worried about.
4. **Watch what happens after a failure.** That's your culture, whatever the values on the wall say.
5. **Rewrite one rule a quarter.** Mindsets follow systems. Start with the rule that punishes the people closest to the customer.
{% /boardcard %}

## Change 27, under new rules

{% fourchairs variant="after" /%}

Nobody in the story became braver. The rules stopped punishing the brave choice. That's what I mean when I say fear is rational: change the rules, and the same rational people will move at the speed you've been asking them for.

*Next in this series: [Fast Edge, Steady Core](/blog/fast-edge-steady-core), on the last wall a change runs into: an architecture that makes small changes wait for the core banking system.*

